            {"speaker": "Stewie", "text": "Peter, a firewall is like a bouncer at a club. It checks who's coming in and who's going out based on rules. But it can't stop someone who's dressed as a legitimate guest."},
            {"speaker": "Peter", "text": "What do you mean?"},
            {"speaker": "Stewie", "text": "Firewalls filter traffic based on rules - IP addresses, ports, protocols. But they can't inspect encrypted traffic without special configuration. The SolarWinds attackers used legitimate HTTPS to exfiltrate data."},
            {"speaker": "Stewie", "text": "There are different types: packet filtering firewalls check headers, stateful firewalls track connection states, and next-gen firewalls can inspect application content."},
            {"speaker": "Peter", "text": "So I need a next-gen one?"},
            {"speaker": "Stewie", "text": "Not necessarily. The best firewall is useless if the rules are wrong. I've seen companies with default 'allow all' rules. Configuration matters more than the product."},
            {"speaker": "Stewie", "text": "Host-based firewalls protect individual machines. Network firewalls protect the perimeter. You need both. Defense in depth."},
            {"speaker": "Peter", "text": "What about cloud firewalls?"},
            {"speaker": "Stewie", "text": "Cloud firewalls, or WAFs, protect web applications. They filter HTTP traffic and can block SQL injection, XSS, and other application-layer attacks. Every production web app needs one."},
            {"speaker": "Stewie", "text": "Interview tip: explain the difference between stateful and stateless firewalls, and when you'd use each. Also know how firewalls can be bypassed."},
